𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your Kubernetes application is experiencing performance issues, and some Pods are getting OOMKilled. How will you troubleshoot CPU and memory requests and limits?”

In production, I wouldn't randomly increase resource limits. First, I'd understand how the application is using its resources.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Check current resource usage

kubectl top pods -n <namespace>
kubectl top nodes

Identify Pods consuming unusually high CPU or memory.

2️⃣ Inspect Pod events

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for "OOMKilled", resource pressure and container termination details.

3️⃣ Review requests and limits

kubectl get deployment <deployment-name> -n <namespace> -o yaml

Check the container's CPU and memory configuration.

🔹 Requests — Resources Kubernetes uses when scheduling Pods.

🔹 Limits — Maximum CPU or memory a container can use.

4️⃣ Understand CPU vs. memory behavior

⚡ CPU limits can throttle a container when it reaches its limit.

🧠 Exceeding a container's memory limit can cause it to be terminated with "OOMKilled".

5️⃣ Compare usage with configuration

Check whether resource requests are too low, memory usage is growing over time, or traffic has increased.

6️⃣ Apply the right fix

Adjust requests and limits based on monitoring data, application requirements and available cluster capacity. Investigate possible memory leaks before simply increasing memory.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

Resource requests affect scheduling. Limits control resource consumption.

Correct resource configuration improves workload stability, but blindly increasing limits can hide the real problem.
